                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="org_337">London. Franciscan
                           convent</orgName><p><q>Istum librum fecit ligari frater Andreas Bavard
                           custos librarie a. d. 1468 de communibus elemosinis prefate librarie
                           collatis</q> (fol. 8v). The London Franciscans had such a book Leland,
                        Collect., 4. 51.</p><note type="MLGB3_medievalCatalogueNotes">?
                        Leland</note></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_94">Cambridge,
                        Cambridgeshire, Franciscan convent</orgName>
                     <p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of
                        Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the Supplement (1987). The
                        corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index card records the
                        following context for the rejection:</p><p>On Andrew Bavard see Kingsford,
                           <title>Grey Friars of London</title>, BSFS 6, pp. 23, 60 etc., where it
                        is assumed that the library in question is at Cambridge. Bavard's later
                        associations are with the London Franciscans. London convent 1460, Oxford
                        convent 1478, London convent 1494 till death (1508) according to Emden,
                           <title>BRUO</title>, p. 133.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">Cardinal
                        Sirleto</persName> (d. 1575) no. 355.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">Duke Giov. Angelo di
                        Altemps</persName>.</provenance>
